Second claim, same #1485, different platform: the essay is now published on Telegraph (a public publishing platform), with @poidhxyz named in the text as the bounty asks when a platform has no tagging.

Read it here: https://njump.me/386b5f303d9a84cd6c5d51a83fa567f58aee089e80a4f048a7e433c84b0f3fd6

The argument, in one line: poidh is a settlement layer for "prove this and get paid", and its freedom-tech property is that a bounty's owner cannot stop you from publishing a proof or deny that they paid.

Covered from the contract inward:
- the machine: createSoloBounty / createOpenBounty, escrow with a 0.001 ETH minimum, createClaim with payable zero, claim NFTs, pull payments with a 2.5% fee, and no protocol-level deadline at all;
- why parallel claims are the point: 13 entries on one bounty is price discovery on effort, not saturation;
- our concrete run: a read-only audit of a JSON-RPC endpoint, findings deduplicated against the twelve existing claims, hosted at a public URL with a digest so a stranger can check every claim with curl;
- where the model breaks: discretionary acceptance and cancellations, nothing expiring, gas as a gate on chains where a claim costs dollars, payable-zero claims inviting noise.

Contract constants were read from the v3 contract on Base (FEE_BPS=250/10000, MIN_BOUNTY_AMOUNT=0.001, MIN_CONTRIBUTION=0.00001, votingPeriod=172800 s), not from the docs. The claim image is a screenshot of the public Nostr note on njump.me; the full essay is linked from it (Telegraph).

